A||||6||WisconsinGT||#2 Cylinder help||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||08-04-2000||06:24 PM||||Where to begin....I just got back from my local GM dealer. I brought the car in to have it looked at because it has been running rich. They called me last week and said that I have two fuel injectors that are bad. I asked them to give them to me, so I could get them balanced and blueprinted. No Problem. Well, I walk in to pick-up my car only to hear the guy at the counter say, "Got some bad news." So, with an open mind I say whats wrong. He yakes me to the mechanic who says, "Yeah, I thought it was the injectors causing the rich mixture. But I see your #2 cylinder is not working." He then runs a test with this prod thing that he sticks on the #2 spark wire hood going to the Distributor. Nothing happens. Then he sicks it on a different hood the engine stumbles. He also mentioned that he ran a compression test. All of the cylinders were getting around 105 to 110. But the #2 cylinder was at 50. While cranking the engine the #2 cylinder was hitting the 100 mark. But after startup it would go down to 50. To make matters worse, I had to pay around $450.00 in labor and injector cleaning cost. This is getting ridiculous! He thinks it is the cam that is the problem. But also mentioned that it could be a valve. Can anyone offer any advise? Is there anything I can do to see if this is really the problem? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||EdsB52||08-05-2000||02:24 AM||||Let's face it. As much as I love Fieros, they're old, used up pieces of sh!t. Name a car that is between 16 and 12 years old that is reliable every day. Maybe a Mercedes, but they generally don't attract the crazy mofos like us that run the crap out of them! (Tangent) I used to own a few GSXRs, and it wouldn't be uncommon to find one, two or three years old that wasn't totalled and blew smoke or needed much repair.<P>Major premise: running the crap out of machines will obviously reduce vehicle life and mandate often or extensive maintenance.<P>Minor premise: Fieros are run hard more often than not.<P>Conclusion: Fieros need more maintenance than most other vehicles.<P>Where is this going you ask. You can chase gremlins for several thousand dollars and still own a problem child, or you can tear it down, change virtually everything, and know what you have. Fieros are not for the white-collar office boy who wants to own a cool car. They're high maintenance pains in the ass that are really fun to drive, and with constant maintenance, give a high return in the form of driving enjoyment.<P>If you're having a sucession of problems, yank the motor and go through it. If you don't have the capacity (workspace, tools, and knowledge), then sell it and but a Jap car. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||08-05-2000||09:55 AM||||Ahhh...I will try and handle this in my best white-collar mindset. Thank you for the black & white advice Eds. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||08-05-2000||11:50 AM||||My car is making some nasty bearing noise and it's getting worse. BUT, think of this as an oppurtunity to make your car a real screamer.  Build yourself a sooped-up engine that you can be proud of. Lets face it, in stock form even the V6's aren't real crazy fast unless you spend lots of $ tweakin.  Hell, for 150 bucks more than what you paid for the injector cleaning, you could get yourself a remanufactured 3.1 shortblock to stick in.  If you have a good mechanical understanding, you can do it yourself.  Just read-up and take your time.  And you've got the forum on your side as well.  At least the car is somewhat driveable while you get a motor together.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-05-2000||12:13 PM||||Hope you didn't take offense to my little rant, as none was intended WisconsinGt. I just feel that for me, there's a threshold where I throw the towel in and rebuild. The older I get, the shorter that threshold becomes. Good luck my friend.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WisconsinGT||08-05-2000||12:24 PM||||THanks for clarifying Eds. Yeah, I have had the towel in my hand for eight months now. I guess I have to make a decision sooner then later. I am always looking for a way to get another year out of my GT. I guess I could drive it until a rod goes. Anyway, I am not looking for big speed gains. (HP) I just want a reliable engine that can give me years of excitement. Since I am mechanically challenged, I have a hard time finding someone I can trust in my area to do this rebuild. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||08-05-2000||12:29 PM||||In an effort to actually address the issue....the first thing that came to my mind was the valve spring or lifter. My thought was, with the car just turning over, the oil pressure is lower, during which state the valve might be operating correctly. With the engine running, the higher oil pressure could be overcoming the valve spring, making it stay open longer, or the lifter could be sticking. Maybe a long shot, but it made sense to me. But then, I understand the theory of relativity too, if that tells you where MY mind resides.....(in The Far Side, in case you weren't following along.....)<P>-Taiji||||reg||3
